cont771 vit CONCLUSION (deep lever, DEFERRED): existing IREE_METAL_COOP_VEC_NONMULT fix does NOT help vit — T=577 fwd+bwd is 0.94 (OFF) vs 0.92 (ON), within noise. That fix only covers the innermost PARALLEL dim of vectorizable transpose/elementwise ops; vit's cost is the fused-matmul BACKWARD (decompose: 71% bwd) whose 577 reduction dim + fused-generic shape route to setDefaultOpConfig with degenerate tiling. Real fix = non-mult REDUCTION-dim tiling in setDefaultOpConfig, or dispatch-formation to keep the matmul un-fused so it reaches setMatmulOpConfig. Both deep/multi-session. Build tree reverted+restored clean; deployed dylib untouched all session. Board stays 67.1% / 0/10 / all-correct. Net session: flash closed (bf16-dead), vit fully root-caused + deferred. Next lever candidates: (a) setDefault non-mult reduction tiling for vit (+3.3% ceiling), (b) decoder cluster 4/10 @ 62-63% (whole-graph backward fusion, research-scoped).
cont772 vit ROOT CAUSE CORRECTED (real dump of the L=1 model, 122 dispatches): the attention MATMULS are ALREADY padded 577->592 (batch_matmul 96x592x592x64 etc.) and FFN matmuls are clean (4624x768x3072). The cost is MATERIALIZED SOFTMAX-BACKWARD generics on raw 577: dispatch_106_matvec_like_96x577x577 = a 96x577x577 (32M-elt) fused generic computing the attn-prob gradient (elementwise scale + 577-reduction for the softmax jacobian + ds=P*(dP-D)*scale), plus reduction_55392x577. These memory-bound 577^2 materialized attn-backward dispatches ARE the 71%-backward gap. => vit's gap is the ATTENTION-MATERIALIZATION / backward-FUSION gap (what jax-metal wins with fused flash-style attn backward), amplified by the larger seqlen — NOT a prime-tiling bug (that was a red herring; matmuls are padded). Same deep class as the decoder backward-fusion gap. DEFERRED as the core research lever.
cont773 flash-on-vit test (aligned T=576 B=6 bf16, MAX_FLASH_SEQ raised): INCORRECT — flash GNORM 9.99e13 vs stock 2.88e5 (garbage). The non-causal kernel is only validated at T=512; at T=576 (9 query-blocks) it breaks => kernel has a seqlen-dependent bug beyond T=512, on top of bf16-at-scale nan + the 577 boundary. So flash-for-encoders is NOT a near-term lever for vit. CONCLUSION: vit's gap (materialized attn-backward fusion) needs either (a) compiler-native attention-backward fusion, or (b) a substantially hardened flash backward (correct at arbitrary aligned seqlens + bf16-scale-stable + 577 boundary). Both deep/multi-session. flash_shim MAX_FLASH_SEQ made env-overridable (harmless, default 512). Board 67.1%/0-of-10/all-correct; deployed dylib untouched.
cont774 FRONTIER FULLY MAPPED (dependency chain for the compiler-native attn-fusion lever): vit emits 0 iree_linalg_ext.attention ops — the JAX frontend MATERIALIZES attention (dot_general + softmax generics like dispatch_106). So closing the gap needs BOTH: (1) RAISE materialized softmax(QK)V -> attention op — raiseFlashAttentionFwd exists but is FORWARD-ONLY (gated IREE_METAL_COOP_RAISE_FLASH); NO backward raiser, and backward = 71% of the vit gap; (2) make SPIRVVectorDistributeAttention PERFORMANT — gated IREE_METAL_COOP_ATTENTION_WIP, compiles but no thread/subgroup distribution (needs the LLVMGPU vector-distribute -> SPIR-V port, per the in-code comment: "major codegen port, not a wiring job"). This IS the campaign core lever [[nlearn-coopmatrix-metal]]. Net: board 67.1% frontier = attention-backward fusion; the two concrete sub-levers are (a) a backward flash-raise pass, (b) the vector-distribute SPIR-V port. Multi-session. Session end state: deployed dylib untouched, source clean, flash chapter closed, vit accurately root-caused + roadmapped.
cont775 flash-shim blocker NARROWED precisely: kernel is f32-EXACT at ALL seqlens — fwd rel-err s512/576/640 = 2.4/2.7/1.7e-7; bwd dQ/dK/dV s512/576 all ~2-5e-7. So vit T=576 garbage (9.99e13) is NOT a seqlen kernel bug — it's the SAME bf16-at-scale divergence, triggered at lower batch for larger T (T=576 => B=6 fails; T=512 => needs B=8), consistent with statistical-overflow-scales-with-total-attention-work. => the flash-shim shortcut's ONLY blocker everywhere is bf16 BACKWARD numerical stability (mathematically correct, f32 bit-exact, but bf16 full-model backward diverges non-deterministically at large T*B*L). Both paths to killing materialized attn-backward are now mapped: (A) compiler-native attn-op+pipeline port (deep), (B) flash-shim (blocked ONLY by bf16-bwd stability; if fixed, test if vit flips positive since its materialized path is far worse than bert's -8%). (B)'s bf16 fix is the more contained next lever.
cont776 FLASH-SHIM CONCLUSIVELY DEAD for the board (vit confirmed): vit T=576 B=2 bf16 flash is CORRECT (GNORM 9.8323e4 vs stock 9.8336e4) but throughput 1.41 vs stock 1.43 = -1.4% (neutral). So even on vit — where the materialized backward is worst — flash does NOT win at configs where it's numerically correct, AND it's bf16-broken exactly at the worst config (B=8 T=577) where it might help. Root: the flash kernel runs attention in f32 internally (slower than stock's bf16 matrix-unit matmuls), offsetting the backward-materialization savings. Pattern holds across bert (-8%), vit (-1.4%), non-causal generally. => the ONLY viable path to the attention-backward-fusion gap is COMPILER-NATIVE (keeps bf16 + matrix units): the attention-op raise (incl. backward) + SPIRVVectorDistributeAttention vector-distribute SPIR-V port. Flash-shim chapter fully closed. flash_shim MAX_FLASH_SEQ left env-overridable (harmless).
cont777 flash-shim closure CONFIRMED with batch trend: vit T=576 flash = -1.4% (B=2) -> -6.0% (B=4, GNORM 1.933987e5 vs stock 1.934146e5 correct), converging to bert's -8% at B=8. Flash gets monotonically WORSE with batch (more f32-attention vs stock's bf16 matrix units). Definitive: flash-shim cannot be a board lever for ANY model. Frontier fully mapped; only compiler-native bf16 coop-matrix fused attention remains (task #38). Diagnostic phase complete.
cont778 CORE-LEVER DEV HARNESS ESTABLISHED (model-free): fork iree-compile/iree-opt/iree-benchmark-module/iree-run-module ARE built in the build tree. Seeded iree_linalg_ext.attention op (96x640x64 bf16, canonical syntax from LinalgExt/IR/test/roundtrip.mlir) COMPILES through the WIP pipeline (IREE_METAL_COOP_ATTENTION_WIP=1, /tmp/attn_op.mlir -> /tmp/attn_op.vmfb). BENCHMARK baseline: 2244 ms (iree-benchmark-module --device=metal) — ~1000x too slow, exactly as documented (M=1 unrolled, no thread/subgroup distribution, no coop-matrix). Confirms blocker = the LLVMGPU vector-distribute -> SPIR-V port (thread/subgroup distribution + coop routing of per-tile matmuls); the IREE_METAL_COOP_ATTN_M knob can't help (code: large M explodes code size without distribution). Harness ready for the port's compile+measure iteration loop. Next: port/wire thread distribution so the per-tile QK/PV matmuls use bf16 WMMA (simdgroup_matrix) + measure against this 2244ms baseline.
cont779 WIP attention MSL codegen DEFICIENCY pinpointed (hard evidence, 204KB/1333-line kernel): 0 simdgroup_matrix ops, 0 per-thread indexing (kernel takes ONLY gl_WorkGroupID [[threadgroup_position_in_grid]] — SINGLE-THREADED per workgroup!), 2791 unrolled float4 ops, 1 K2 reduction loop. => each workgroup serially computes its whole M-tile x K2 in scalar float4, no matrix units. This IS why 2244ms. Fix = (1) thread/subgroup distribution (spread M-tile+K2 across the 32 simd lanes), (2) route QK/PV per-tile matmuls to simdgroup_matrix. Next: find SPIRVVectorDistributeAttention pipeline pass-list vs LLVMGPU vector-distribute to see which distribution passes are missing.
cont780 CORE-LEVER port defects PINPOINTED (existing gated coop-attn experiments tested on the harness, all vs 2244ms baseline): (1) IREE_METAL_COOP_ATTN_THREAD=1 -> COMPILE FAIL "failed to legalize scf.forall" with mapping=[#gpu.thread<linear_dim_0>] upperBound=1 — the thread-tiling emits an scf.forall(thread) but the THREAD-only branch (Passes.cpp:870-877) lacks a forall-resolution/GPUDistribute pass (the COOPSMEM branch has one post-bufferize). Also mBlock=1 default => degenerate 1-iteration forall; need IREE_METAL_COOP_ATTN_M>=subgroup to actually fill lanes. (2) IREE_METAL_COOP_ATTN_COOPSMEM=1 -> COMPILE FAIL "expected lowering config on a (batch) matmul op" — the decomposed qk/pv matmuls carry NO lowering_config so the coop tiling can't fire. Concrete port TODOs: (a) add scf.forall(thread)->threads resolution to the THREAD branch + set M>=32; (b) propagate/attach a coop lowering_config onto the post-decompose qk/pv matmuls. Both in SPIRV/Passes.cpp + KernelConfig.cpp. Harness (compile+iree-benchmark-module) ready to measure each fix vs 2244ms.
cont781 BREAKTHROUGH on core lever: IREE_METAL_COOP_ATTN_SMEM=1 + IREE_METAL_COOP_ATTN_M=32 => 70.5ms vs 2244ms naive WIP = 32x. The thread-distributed flash (M=32 = 1 query/subgroup-lane, ConfigSoftmaxThreadsPass + Thread-tiling + GPUDistribute, Passes.cpp SMEM branch) COMPILES + RUNS. SMEM M=1=1676ms. First positive result on the attention-fusion lever. Next: materialized-attn reference (no-WIP) for 96x640x64 to see if flash BEATS it; M sweep; +COOPSMEM coop-matrix.
cont782 CORE-LEVER two precise results: (1) thread-distributed flash SMEM_M32 = 70.5ms WORKS (M=32=subgroup width; M=64 fails "required parallel resources (blocks)" = exceeds 32 lanes). ~71 GFLOP/s fwd — 32x over naive but NOT matrix-units yet. (2) coop-matrix path COOPSMEM: COMPILES + generates coop ops but SPIRV-CROSS emits INVALID MSL at runtime: "invalid operands to binary expression ('spvCoopMat<float,4>' and 'spvCoopMat<float,4>')" (metal executable.m:232 MTLLibrary create fail). => the matrix-unit attention is blocked at the SPIRV-Cross coop-matrix->MSL emission (spvCoopMat binary op malformed) — the exact [[nlearn-coopmatrix-metal]] / jax-apple-demand SPIRV-Cross gap. third_party/spirv_cross already shows as fork-modified. NEXT: inspect the generated MSL around the spvCoopMat binary expr + the SPIRV-Cross coop-matrix emitter; that fix unlocks matrix-unit fused attention. Harness (compile+bench seeded attention op) proven to iterate this in ~1min/variant, no full model/build needed.
cont783 ROOT BUG FULLY ISOLATED — the exact terminus of the campaign lever chain (gap -> attn-bwd fusion -> coop-matrix fused attn -> SPIRV-Cross MSL). The emitted spvCoopMat MSL struct = { simdgroup_matrix<T,8,8> t[N]; spvCoopMat(T v) fill-ctor; operator[](uint) }. It has NO operator* / matmul. But SPIRV-Cross lowers OpCooperativeMatrixMulAdd to a plain "A * B" (e.g. `auto _coopst = _52988 * _52991;`) => MTLLibrary compile error "invalid operands to binary expression ('spvCoopMat<float,4>' x 2)". FIX (third_party/spirv_cross spirv_msl.cpp): make the coop-matrix multiply emit fragment-grid simdgroup_multiply_accumulate — either add spvCoopMat::operator* (needs the 2D fragment shape M/N/K, not just flat N) or emit a spvCoopMatMulAdd(A,B,C) helper looping simdgroup_multiply_accumulate over the (M/8)x(N/8)x(K/8) fragment grid. This unlocks matrix-unit fused attention (COOPSMEM path already generates the coop ops + simdgroup_load/store correctly; only the MULTIPLY emission is malformed). Thread-distributed flash (SMEM_M32=70.5ms) works as the interim non-coop path. Harness iterates in ~1min/variant.
cont784 fix REFINED (read spirv_msl.cpp): the OpCooperativeMatrixMulAddKHR handler (line 9467) is ALREADY CORRECT — loops tm x tn x tk emitting simdgroup_multiply_accumulate(D[i*tn+j], A[i*tk+k], B[k*tn+j], D). So the invalid "A * B" in the MSL is NOT from MulAdd — it's a DIFFERENT coop-matrix multiply op (plain OpMatrixTimesMatrix / OpFMul / OpVectorTimesMatrix on coop-matrix operands) that SPIRV-Cross emits via its DEFAULT binary-op path (bare "*") without the tile-loop, because spvCoopMat has no operator*. Root: IREE's coop-attn lowering produces a plain matrix-multiply (not a MulAdd/contract) somewhere in the qk/pv/softmax chain, and SPIRV-Cross doesn't special-case coop operands for that opcode. FIX: in spirv_msl.cpp, detect coop-matrix operands in the OpMatrixTimesMatrix/OpFMul emission and route to the same tile-loop simdgroup_multiply_accumulate (C=0 seed) as MulAdd. NEXT: dump the coop-pipeline SPIR-V (--iree-hal-dump-executable-intermediates) to identify which exact opcode produces the bare '*', then add that case. Thread-flash SMEM_M32=70.5ms remains the working interim path.
cont785 EXACT coop blocker found (SPIR-V dialect dump, line 56751): %828 = spirv.FMul %820, %823 : !spirv.coopmatrix<16x16xf32, Subgroup, MatrixAcc> — an ELEMENTWISE FMul on TWO coop-matrix ACCUMULATORS (a softmax intermediate), NOT a matmul. This is why SPIRV-Cross emits bare "A*B": FMul is elementwise. DEEPER TRUTH: Metal simdgroup_matrix has NO elementwise multiply (its operator* IS matmul), so a coop-matrix Hadamard product fundamentally cannot lower to simdgroup_matrix — adding spvCoopMat::operator* would WRONGLY emit per-fragment matmul. So the fix is NOT in SPIRV-Cross: it's IREE-side — the COOPSMEM path must keep the softmax elementwise intermediates OUT of coop layout (its stated intent: stage scores in smem, softmax as a normal smem reduction). Some elementwise softmax op leaked into coop-matrix form. NEXT: find where the coop-attn tiling assigns coop layout to the softmax generics (should be regular vector); likely SPIRVTileToCooperativeOps / VectorizeToCooperative over-capturing the softmax. Thread-flash SMEM_M32=70.5ms stays the working coop-free interim.
cont786 DECISIVE strategic result: MATERIALIZED attention (f32, 96x640x64) = 23.1ms, vs thread-flash SMEM_M32 (bf16) = 70.5ms => materialized is 3x FASTER. Why: the materialized path already runs its QK/PV dot_generals on MATRIX UNITS (coop matmul), only the T×T softmax is memory-bound; the thread-flash does SCALAR matmuls (no coop) so it loses despite avoiding materialization. => thread-flash is a DEAD interim (not deployable, 3x worse than status quo); COOP-MATRIX is MANDATORY for fused flash to win. The singular lever is unchanged and confirmed: fix the COOPSMEM softmax-elementwise-in-coop-layout leak (cont785) so the fused flash's qk/pv use simdgroup_matrix while softmax stays in regular layout. TARGET: coop-flash must beat 23.1ms (and ideally jax-metal's fused attn). Back to the coop-layout fix as the ONLY path.
cont787 FIX TARGET surgically pinpointed (SPIR-V context of the failing FMul): the pattern is CooperativeMatrixLoad(smem) x2 -> spirv.FMul (elementwise) -> CooperativeMatrixStore(smem) — a pure ELEMENTWISE smem multiply (softmax term / normalization) that was WRONGLY given coop-matrix layout. It needs NO coop (regular vector load/mul/store). Root: the SPIRV coop-vectorization OVER-CAPTURES an elementwise op that shares smem buffers with the qk/pv matmuls. FIX (IREE-side, surgical): in SPIRVVectorizeToCooperativeOps (and/or the coop TileToCooperative), restrict coop layout to CONTRACTION ops (vector.contract) only; leave elementwise vector ops (transfer_read/arith.mulf/transfer_write) in regular vector layout. Then the qk/pv use simdgroup_matrix, softmax stays regular. TARGET beat 23.1ms materialized. Harness ready. NEXT: read createSPIRVVectorizeToCooperativeOpsPass to see its op-selection + add the contraction-only guard.
cont788 CAMPAIGN TERMINUS pinpointed to one codegen decision: SPIRVTileAndVectorizeToCooperativeOps.cpp getCooperativeOpVectorShape() line 208 unrolls ELEMENTWISE ops to the coop native 16x16 shape -> MLIR VectorToGPU then converts them to coop-matrix elementwise (subgroup_mma_elementwise, valid on NVIDIA WMMA) -> on METAL simdgroup_matrix has NO elementwise op -> SPIRV-Cross emits invalid "A*B". THE fix (Metal-specific, the research-grade Apple simdgroup-layout wall the COOPSMEM path meant to sidestep): keep elementwise vector ops in REGULAR vector layout (do NOT give them coop native shape / do NOT let VectorToGPU coop-ify them); only vector.contract -> coop; softmax loads/stores through smem stay regular. Reduces the whole 33%-to-parity gap to: "make the Metal coop path leave elementwise softmax ops in regular vector layout." Harness + 23.1ms target ready. Implementation = deep VectorToGPU/coop-vectorize change w/ build+test cycles (the real multi-session work, now surgically scoped).
cont789 fix DEPTH confirmed (read getCooperativeOpVectorShape 202-269): the elementwise branch (208-213) returns coop native 16x16 shape as a PREREQUISITE for VectorToGPU coop-conversion; returning nullopt would leave huge un-unrolled vectors (the documented hang). So the fix is NOT here — it's in VectorToGPU's elementwise handling for the METAL target: leave elementwise ops as REGULAR thread-distributed vectors (Metal simdgroup_matrix has no elementwise) while only vector.contract -> coop, threading the layout boundary through the smem-staged scores. = the research-grade Apple simdgroup-composite-layout problem [[nlearn-coopmatrix-metal]]. CAMPAIGN fully reduced: 33%-to-parity gap == this one research-grade Metal coop/elementwise layout-boundary fix. Complete toolkit in place: harness (compile+bench seeded attn op ~1min/variant), 23.1ms materialized target, working coop-free interim (thread-flash 70.5ms), exact code sites (VectorToGPU elementwise-for-Metal; SPIRVTileAndVectorizeToCooperativeOps line 208; spirv_msl.cpp coop emitters already correct for contractions). Diagnostic campaign COMPLETE; remainder is the scoped research/impl effort.
cont790 COMPILE BLOCKER SOLVED: NLEARN_NO_COOP_ELEMENTWISE=1 (already-existing gated fix in VectorToGPU elementwiseSupportsMMAMatrixType — was OFF by default + the bf16-only guard missed the f32 softmax FMul) makes COOPSMEM COMPILE (no invalid spvCoopMat elementwise). But perf=2998ms with M=32 (42x worse than thread-flash) — the M=32 THREAD-tiling conflicts with COOPSMEM's SUBGROUP-cooperative coop matmuls. Now a perf-tuning problem, not correctness. Testing coop configs w/o the thread-tile conflict.
cont791 DECISIVE (measure>predict, redirects the "coop-flash is THE lever" conclusion): with the compile fixed (NLEARN_NO_COOP_ELEMENTWISE=1), COOPSMEM coop-flash = 2998ms (M=32) / 3846ms (M=16) / compile-fail (M=1, no config) — ALL 40-160x SLOWER than materialized 23.1ms and 40-55x slower than thread-flash 70.5ms. => the coop-matrix FUSED-attention scaffolding does NOT beat the fork's OWN materialized attention (which already runs qk/pv on matrix units, only softmax memory-bound). Fused flash is a DEAD END at this shape. TWO corrections: (a) coop-flash is not viable as-is (perf, not just compile); (b) my harness benches the FORWARD attention op, but vit's actual gap is the BACKWARD (dispatch_106 materialized softmax-jacobian, 71% of gap) — which has NO attention op (raiser is fwd-only), so the fwd harness never addressed the real gap. Updated picture: fork materialized attn (23.1ms) is already its best; jax-metal's edge is better materialized/backward codegen, NOT a flash structure the fork can reach. Next: benchmark jax-metal's isolated attention at this shape (is 23.1ms already close?) + get a BACKWARD-attention repro to test the actual gap.
cont792 vit backward bottleneck characterized: dispatch_106 (softmax-jacobian 96x577x577, the 71%-backward op) config = SPIRVSubgroupReduce wg[32,1,1] tile[[1,1],[0,0,32]] — one subgroup per (head,query) row, 32-wide reduce over 577. REASONABLE tiling, NOT degenerate. It's a legitimately MEMORY-BOUND materialized op (~256MB traffic over the 96x577x577 P/dP/ds matrices). So the vit gap is NOT a fixable tiling bug — it's that jax-metal FUSES the attention backward (flash, never materializes the 577^2), and the fork's fused-flash paths are all SLOWER than its own materialized path (cont791). CAMPAIGN HONEST STATE: the remaining 33%-to-parity is fundamentally jax-metal's superior attention FUSION (fwd+bwd, avoiding materialization); the fork's coop/thread flash scaffolding cannot match it (130x too slow) and flash-shim is bf16-dead. This is a research-grade attention-fusion gap the current fork tooling can't crack. All cheap/tractable levers exhausted+banked this session (flash, tiling, coop-flash all dead-ends, measured). Board 67.1% stable, 10/10 correct, deployed dylib untouched.
cont793 CONFIRMED attention IS vit's bottleneck (time-scaling, not assumed): vit fork fwd+bwd raw ms vs T (B=4 L=12 bf16): T=64:128.5 T=128:187.2 T=256:363.3 — super-linear deltas (+58.7,+176.1) => T^2. Fit time ~= 89ms(fixed) + 0.46*T + 0.0024*T^2. At vit's real T=577: attention(T^2)=~796ms = 69% of ~1149ms total, matching the 71%-backward decompose. So the gap IS attention-materialization (verified, not assumed) — the measure>predict check confirmed rather than overturned it. Secondary: 89ms fixed dispatch overhead (only 8% at T=577) + 0.46*T linear (FFN/elementwise, ~23%). Definitive: vit needs attention FUSION (flash), which every fork path is measured slower at. All non-attention levers would touch <31% of the gap. Frontier fully confirmed; remaining = research-grade attention fusion.
cont794 CRITICAL CORRECTION (cont793 was WRONG — measure>predict catches it): added T=512 -> full curve 64:128.5 128:187.2 256:363.3 512:723.3. delta(256->512)/delta(128->256) = 360/176 = 2.04 ~= LINEAR (2.0), NOT quadratic (4.0). 4-point fit: time ~= 57 + 1.09*T + 0.0004*T^2. At T=577: LINEAR per-token=77%, attention(T^2)=ONLY 17%, fixed=7%. My cont793 3-point fit overfit T^2 on the small-T fixed-overhead curvature. => ATTENTION IS NOT vit's primary bottleneck — even perfect attn fusion saves ~17%. The gap is dominated by PER-TOKEN work: FFN matmuls + elementwise + LayerNorm backward + dispatch overhead (linear in T). This REOPENS tractable levers (FFN/elementwise/LN codegen, dispatch count) that I wrongly dismissed while chasing attention fusion for ~15 turns. The whole flash/coop-attention deep-dive addressed a 17% slice, not the 77%. NEXT: profile vit's per-token BACKWARD dispatches (FFN matmul tiling 4624x768x3072, elementwise/LN fusion, dispatch count) to find the real slow ones.
cont795 per-token breakdown (measured): FFN matmul 2048x768x3072 bf16 = 3.31ms (2.9 TFLOP/s, ~40% of bf16 peak — real matrix-unit compute). Rough vit T=512 B=4 (723ms) accounting: ~72 FFN matmuls ~238ms (33%), attn-softmax T^2 ~17%, projections small => ~35% (~250ms) is NON-matmul NON-attention: elementwise/LayerNorm/transpose/dispatch overhead. THIS is the tractable fusion lever (dispatch census: 49 unfused elementwise/transpose/reduction dispatches per L=2 that jax-metal fuses into matmul epilogues). Perfect fusion of the 35% => vit 723->~470ms = 1.54x => 43%->~66% (+2.3% board from vit alone), AND likely helps ALL models (same unfused-dispatch pattern). Reopened by the cont794 attention-isnt-dominant correction. NEXT: categorize vit's non-matmul dispatches (transposes/elementwise/LN) to find fusable epilogues vs genuine separate ops.
cont796 transpose insight reconciles the campaign: the 24 transposes/layer are the attention HEAD-FOLD reshapes (reshape(B,T,H,D).transpose(0,2,1,3)) — LINEAR/per-token, part of the 77%. This is what flash was documented to eliminate (its bwd win = killing head-fold transposes, NOT the softmax T^2). So flash's value was transposes (linear), not materialization (17%) — but fork flash is bf16-dead. KEY: those transposes are ALSO reducible by LAYOUT FUSION (IsolateTransposedReads / better matmul operand-layout handling) WITHOUT flash's bf16 problems. Reopened tractable board-wide lever = per-token dispatch fusion: fold the 24 transpose + 40 elementwise/layer into matmul epilogues/layouts. Attention T^2 (17%) deprioritized. NEXT: check IsolateTransposedReads (task#30) status/headroom + test transpose-fusion on vit; measure per-dispatch time to confirm the ~35% overhead is fusable vs inherent-memory-bound.
cont797 transpose breakdown (vit L=1): head-fold (8x577x12x64) + BACKWARD-matmul operand transposes (weight 768x768/3072x768/768x3072, activation 4616x3072/4616x768 for dW=X^T dY, dX=dY W^T). Each transpose ~0.15ms memory-bound => transposes are a dispatch-COUNT issue, NOT time-dominant (~3.6ms/layer total). So the ~34% non-matmul overhead is dominated by ELEMENTWISE/LN memory traffic + DISPATCH-LAUNCH overhead (40 elementwise + 22 reduction/layer), both cut by fusion. Note KernelConfig already accepts transposed matmul operands (IREE_METAL_COOP_NO_TRANSPOSED) so the backward transposes SHOULDN'T need separate dispatches — they're not folding into the consuming matmul (transpose-into-matmul fusion gap). NEXT (needs per-dispatch timing to size precisely, via --iree-hal-dump-executable-benchmarks-to): quantify elementwise/LN vs launch-overhead split; then target epilogue fusion (fold elementwise/LN into matmul dispatches) — the board-wide fusion lever. Board 67.1%, dylib untouched, source clean.
cont798 FFN matmul lever examined: 2048x768x3072 uses SPIRVCooperativeMatrixVectorize (coop matrix units, 4129 coop ops) at 2.9 TFLOP/s (~40% peak) but pipeline_depth=0 (NO software pipelining). ROOT: forced by Metal's 32KB shared-mem limit — the [128,64]/K=64 tile is ~27KB single-buffered, double-buffering (~54KB, needed for pipelining) exceeds 32KB so KernelConfig drives depth->0 (line 1167-1171). Not a bug — the pipelining-vs-tile tradeoff under Apple's tight shared mem (NVIDIA has more, where this pipeline was tuned). Improving = smaller-tile-pipelined config (uncertain net win, deep tuning). CAMPAIGN HONEST STATE after full landscape map: vit gap splits ~17% attention (research-grade flash, fork can't), ~33% FFN matmul (coop units, pipelining blocked by 32KB smem), ~34% elementwise/LN/transpose (fusion; fork already aggressive-fuses, remainder hard). EVERY lever hits a real Apple constraint or is deep multi-week work; NO quick board win. Gap to jax-metal(MPSGraph, Apple-tuned) is broad-based codegen quality vs Apple's own highly-tuned kernels. Board 67.1% stable, 10/10 correct, dylib untouched, zero regressions all session.
cont799 last ready gated lever tested — BMM_BOOST is a DEAD END: vit no-op (0.94 off vs 0.93-0.94 boost2/4, seqlen 592 fails the tile-grow divisibility); gpt2-small T=512 = -4% (2.04 off -> 1.95 boost4). Correctness held (GNORM identical). So task#26's BMM_BOOST doesn't move the board (gated off for reason). COMPLETE lever inventory now EXHAUSTED with measurements: flash-shim(dead), coop-flash(130x slow), attention-fusion(17% ceiling, research-grade), FFN-matmul-pipelining(blocked by Apple 32KB smem), BMM_BOOST(no-op/-4%), dispatch-fusion(already aggressive-fused). Board 67.1% is genuinely stuck without deep multi-week codegen work vs Apple's tuned MPSGraph. Every tractable-seeming lever verified dead/constrained. NO quick win exists. Session delivered: exhaustive measured lever-closure (prevents wasted future effort) + corrected the attention-is-dominant error (it's 17% not 69%) + full gap decomposition. Deployed dylib untouched, source clean @ HEAD, 10/10 correct, zero regressions.
cont800 BMM_BOOST complete data (confirms dead): vit no-op | gpt2-small -4% (2.04->1.95) | bert +1.4% (2.17->2.20, noise-level). MIXED, net neutral-to-negative -> correctly gated off, not a board-wide win. Lever inventory fully exhausted+measured. Campaign at verified terminus: board 67.1%, no quick win, remaining gap = broad-based codegen quality vs Apple-tuned MPSGraph under real HW constraints (32KB smem, no coop elementwise) = dedicated multi-week eng effort, not loop-diagnosis. All dead ends now eliminated with data so the next focused effort starts clean. Deployed dylib untouched, source @ HEAD, 10/10 correct, zero regressions this entire session.
cont801 STRATEGIC REFRAME (matmul-peak sweep): fork coop-matrix matmul PEAKS at ~3.64 TFLOP/s (4096^3; 2048^3=3.27; FFN 2048x768x3072=2.88 = 79% of peak). KEY: board is 67% = 1.49x behind jax-metal; if MPSGraph hits ~5.5 TFLOP/s that's ~1.5x => THE BOARD GAP ~= THE RAW MATMUL-THROUGHPUT GAP. So the PRIMARY lever is NOT attention(17%) or fusion — it's raw coop-matrix matmul codegen EFFICIENCY (fork peak 3.64 -> MPSGraph's higher), which is board-WIDE (matmuls ~50%+ of every model). Two sub-levers: (a) FFN at 79% of fork-peak => ~26% tiling headroom for non-square/small-K shapes; (b) the fork-peak 3.64 itself vs MPSGraph = deep simdgroup_matrix utilization (pipelining blocked by 32KB smem, tile shapes, coop occupancy). This reframes the whole campaign: attention/flash was a sideshow; RAW MATMUL is the gap. NEXT: profile why fork matmul caps at 3.64 (occupancy? pipelining? tile?) vs get MPSGraph's matmul number for calibration. Board 67.1%, dylib untouched.
cont802 DECISIVE CALIBRATION (ran jax-metal directly, ~/.venvs/jaxmetal JAX_PLATFORMS=METAL) — refutes cont801 matmul reframe: jax-metal MATMUL peak = 2048^3:2.17, 4096^3:3.72, FFN:3.12 TFLOP/s. FORK matmul is AT PARITY OR BETTER (3.27/3.64/2.88; fork WINS 2048^3 3.27 vs 2.17). So raw matmul throughput is NOT the gap. Yet whole-model gap is 2.3x (vit 0.94 vs 2.19). Math: matmuls ~parity(~3 TFLOP/s both) but fork model 2.3x slower => NON-MATMUL work is ~84% of fork time vs ~69% of jm; fork spends ~2.86x LONGER in non-matmul (fusion/dispatch/elementwise/transpose/attn-materialize). THE GAP IS FUSION/OVERHEAD, definitively — NOT matmul, NOT attention-per-se. Confirms the cont794-795 dispatch-fusion lever via direct calibration. measure>predict caught it again (predicted jm~5.5, actual 3.7=parity). PRIMARY LEVER CONFIRMED: cut the non-matmul overhead (fuse the 40 elementwise + 24 transpose + reductions/layer into matmul epilogues, kill slow_memcpy) — board-wide, the real root cause. NEXT: find what jax-metal fuses that the fork doesn't (dispatch-count fork vs jm per layer).
cont803 ROOT-CAUSE SYNTHESIS (unifies the campaign): fork FFN matmul dispatch_38 is BARE (fill+matmul, NO fused epilogue). The gap = matmul EPILOGUE FUSION: jax-metal fuses GELU/bias/residual/cast INTO the matmul; the fork emits bare matmul + SEPARATE elementwise dispatches (each a full memory round-trip over the activation) = the measured 2.86x non-matmul overhead = the board gap. CAUSE is the SAME Metal constraint that killed coop-flash: simdgroup_matrix has NO elementwise op, so an epilogue can't run on the coop accumulator -> IREE stores the coop result to memory and does elementwise in a separate dispatch. jax-metal/MPSGraph fuses via post-store (coop result -> regs/smem -> regular elementwise in-kernel). THE board-wide lever: make IREE fuse elementwise epilogues into the coop-matmul dispatch (promote-C store -> regular-vector epilogue in-dispatch, using the NLEARN_NO_COOP_ELEMENTWISE regular-vector path). Ties together: coop-elementwise limit (found via flash) ALSO blocks epilogue fusion = the real gap. Matmul throughput is at parity (cont802); it's the wrapping. NEXT: check IREE FormDispatchRegions / SPIRV coop-matmul epilogue-fusion path + whether a flag enables consumer fusion into coop matmul.
cont804 testing OFF-by-default fusion flags toward the confirmed fusion-overhead lever (propagate-transposes, fuse-horizontal-contractions, element-wise-fuse-multi-reduction) on vit — correctness+throughput A/B running. These directly target the 2.86x non-matmul overhead (transposes/QKV-projections/LN). If any helps + stays correct = cheap board win (compiler-option, no build).
cont805 LEVER PRECISELY CALIBRATED (fusion, not attention/matmul): (1) single-use matmul+GELU FUSES fine (standalone 1 dispatch); (2) multi-use matmul+truncf+return ALSO compiles fine with fuse-multi-use=true; (3) but fuse-multi-use=true on VIT fails metal-spirv codegen at bench.py:63/67 (the ATTENTION mha): "vector.transfer_write rank>1 should be broken down by prior passes" (SPIRVVectorizeLoadStore.cpp:1090). So the fork runs fuse-multi-use=FALSE (disabling most backward-epilogue fusion => bare matmuls => the 2.86x non-matmul overhead = THE board gap) because enabling it breaks metal-spirv codegen on the ATTENTION's fused multi-use pattern (reshape/transpose/softmax), NOT simple FFN epilogues. CAMPAIGN ANSWER (calibrated across cont802-805): gap = IREE multi-use FUSION QUALITY vs MPSGraph; the unlock = fix the metal-spirv rank>1 transfer_write breakdown for the attention multi-use pattern so fuse-multi-use=true compiles => epilogue fusion board-wide. Matmul at parity, attention-materialize only 17%; FUSION is the gap. NEXT: isolate the attention multi-use pattern that trips SPIRVVectorizeLoadStore:1090 (reshape+transpose+softmax fused), fix the breakdown, rebuild, A/B fuse-multi-use=true.
cont806 fuse-multi-use=true DOES fuse epilogues at dispatch-formation (vit dispatch_38 now matmul+generic=2 ops vs bare; 96 dispatches vs 122). Only ONE attention dispatch fails metal-spirv codegen (rank>1 transfer_write). Scanning all 96 dispatches standalone to pinpoint the exact failing one -> then fix its breakdown = unlock board-wide epilogue fusion. The fusion itself works; it's a single localized codegen bug.
cont807 BLOCKER fully localized to ONE dispatch: fuse-multi-use=true fuses ALL FFN/projection epilogues fine; ONLY dispatch_97 (attention softmax-backward matvec_like 96x577x577) fails metal-spirv. Cause: the fused generic has TWO outputs yielding the same value %21 — one normal map (d0,d1,d2), one TRANSPOSED (d0,d2,d1) (dS needed in both [q,k] and [k,q] layouts for dK/dV backward matmuls). The transposed store => rank>1 vector.transfer_write that SPIRVVectorizeLoadStore.cpp:1090 can't vectorize. So the board-wide epilogue-fusion win (fuse-multi-use=true) is blocked by ONE dispatch's fused transposed-output store. FIX (targeted, 2 options): (a) codegen — break down the transposed rank>1 transfer_write (SPIRVBreakDownLargeVector to handle transposed maps), or (b) dispatch-formation — isolate the transposed output so it stays a separate transpose (like IsolateTransposedReads but for writes), keeping dispatch_97 vectorizable while everything else fuses. NEXT: try (b) first (lower-risk) — prevent fusing a transposed output into a multi-output generic; rebuild; A/B fuse-multi-use=true on vit -> measure board win. This is the closest-to-shippable lever of the campaign.
cont808 fix-site scoped: SPIRVVectorizeLoadStore.cpp:1087-1094 is a HARD GATE (fails if any transfer op rank>1, expecting a prior SPIRVBreakDownLargeVector to have broken it down). The transposed-output store (d0,d2,d1) in dispatch_97 isn't broken down by that prior pass (transpose permutation not handled). Both fixes are real compiler changes w/ build cycles: (a) make SPIRVBreakDownLargeVector unroll transposed transfer_writes to rank-1; (b) dispatch-formation: isolate transposed outputs so they don't fuse into a multi-output generic. CAMPAIGN precisely aimed + scoped: gap=fusion quality (measured, matmul-at-parity, attn only 17%); board-wide unlock = enable fuse-multi-use=true, blocked ONLY by dispatch_97's transposed rank>1 store; fix = one of the two above. This is the correct multi-week target, fully localized. Board 67.1%, dylib untouched, source @ HEAD, 10/10 correct, zero regressions.
cont809 fusion-win TEMPERED (measure>predict again): vit FORWARD-ONLY compiles with fuse-multi-use=true (no dispatch_97/backward) => 1.11 vs 1.10 TFLOP/s = +0.9% (noise). So forward epilogue fusion barely helps. Backward win unmeasurable without the dispatch_97 codegen fix (catch-22). Weak forward signal suggests much of the measured 2.86x non-matmul overhead is INHERENT memory-bound ops (LayerNorm reductions, softmax materialization, residual adds) NOT fusable matmul-epilogues => fuse-multi-use may be a SMALL win, not the board-mover hoped. HONEST STATE: gap IS non-matmul overhead (confirmed, matmul-at-parity), but its tractability via epilogue fusion is UNCERTAIN (weak fwd evidence); the fusable-epilogue slice appears small and the rest is inherent bandwidth. Closing it likely needs reducing memory traffic on the LN/softmax/residual ops (harder, more like MPSGraph's whole-graph memory planning) not just matmul-epilogue fusion. Deep dispatch_97 fix would let us MEASURE the backward fusion win to decide. Board 67.1%, dylib untouched, source clean.
cont810 per-dispatch auto-bench (--iree-hal-dump-executable-benchmarks-to) needs full-compile not --compile-mode=hal-executable; tooling friction on isolated per-dispatch timing (indirect bindings). CAMPAIGN HONEST TERMINUS after exhaustive measured diagnosis (~138 turns): gap = non-matmul overhead (fork 2.86x vs jm), CONFIRMED matmul-at-parity + attention only 17%; the tractable fusion lever (fuse-multi-use) is WEAK (fwd +0.9%) with the bwd win blocked by dispatch_97's transposed rank>1 transfer_write codegen bug; strong signal the overhead is largely INHERENT memory-bound (LN/softmax/residual traffic) = jax-metal's whole-graph memory planning, a deep multi-week MPSGraph-parity effort with NO quick win. Every specific lever CLOSED with data: flash/coop-flash(dead), attn-fusion(17%), matmul-tune(parity), BMM_BOOST(no-op), epilogue-fusion(weak+blocked). Board 67.1% stable, dylib untouched, source @ HEAD, 10/10 correct, zero regressions. Next real progress = dedicated impl effort (fix dispatch_97 to MEASURE the bwd fusion win as the decisive go/no-go; OR whole-graph memory-traffic reduction), not further loop-diagnosis which now re-derives these verified findings.
cont811 MLP CALIBRATION reframes the gap (corrects cont794): pure-MLP FFN-only (B=8 T=512 L=12, no attention) = 468ms @ 2.97 TFLOP/s ~= matmul peak => FFN+GELU+residual is EFFICIENT. Full vit B=8 T=577 = 2541ms. So FFN is only ~20% of vit; ~80% is the ATTENTION BLOCK (QKV/O projections + QK/PV + softmax-materialization + head-fold transposes). cont794's "attention=17%" was only the T^2 SOFTMAX; the attention's LINEAR parts (transposes/head-folds/projections, linear in T) were mislabeled into the 77% "per-token" term — they're actually attention, NOT FFN. So the gap IS the attention block's non-matmul overhead (transposes/head-folds/materialized-softmax) — exactly what FLASH eliminates. Attention matmuls are at parity (cont802); the overhead is the surrounding transpose/materialize. Full circle but QUANTIFIED: attention overhead ~large (not 17%), flash is the fix, fork flash bf16-dead. => the real lever is eliminating attention transposes/head-folds/materialization WITHOUT flash's bf16 problems (layout fusion / better attention lowering).
cont812 MLP fuse-multi-use=true = 455ms (+3% vs 468, loss-correct) => FFN epilogue fusion is a small +3% win, FFN efficient (~3 TFLOP/s). RECONCILED CAMPAIGN (fully calibrated): vit gap = the ATTENTION BLOCK (~80% of vit time): its matmuls (QK/PV/QKVO-proj) at PARITY (cont802) but the surrounding overhead — head-fold TRANSPOSES + softmax MATERIALIZATION + layout — is the drag. FFN ~20% + efficient. cont794's "attn=17%" undercounted (that was only T^2 softmax; attn-linear transposes/head-folds/projections are the bulk of the 77% "linear" term). Fixes: (a) coop-flash (bf16 matrix-unit flash, eliminates the overhead) = research-grade, blocked by Metal coop-elementwise (130x slow); (b) fork f32-flash = eliminates overhead but its f32 matmuls lose to materialized bf16 (-8%); (c) attention-LAYOUT optimization (propagate-transposes / avoid head-fold transpose) = doesn't need flash, PJRT-plumbing-blocked. NET: the attention-overhead gap needs deep attention-lowering work; matmul + FFN are fine. Board 67.1%. NEXT: get propagate-transposes working (fix PJRT flag plumbing) to test the layout-only attention lever.
cont813 propagate-transposes (attention-layout lever) is PJRT-BLOCKED: "option not found: iree-global-opt-propagate-transposes" — global-opt flags aren't registered in the PJRT compiler options, so the model path can't test it without a PJRT code change (or standalone iree-compile on vit stablehlo). CAMPAIGN FULLY CALIBRATED + all attention-overhead levers scoped/blocked: gap = ATTENTION BLOCK ~80% of vit (transposes/head-folds/softmax-materialization overhead; matmuls at parity, FFN efficient ~20%); fixes: coop-flash(research-grade, Metal coop-elementwise-blocked, 130x), f32-flash(-8%, f32<bf16-matmul), fuse-multi-use(+3% FFN only), propagate-transposes(PJRT-plumbing-blocked). No quick win; the attention-block overhead needs deep attention-lowering work (bf16 matrix-unit flash OR layout-fusion with PJRT plumbing). Board 67.1% stable, dylib untouched, source @ HEAD, 10/10 correct, ZERO regressions across ~150 turns. Exhaustive measured diagnosis complete: matmul+FFN fine, gap is attention non-matmul overhead, all levers deep/blocked. Next real progress = dedicated multi-week attention-lowering effort, precisely aimed + de-risked.
cont814 THE GAP FOUND (corrects cont802 "matmul at parity"): BATCHED small-K ATTENTION matmuls are 6-7x slower than jax-metal. QK 96x577x64x577: fork 24.4ms/0.17 TFLOP/s vs jm 3.36ms/1.22 = 7.3x slower. PV 96x577x577x64: fork 13.6ms/0.30 vs jm 2.23ms/1.84 = 6.1x. cont802 only tested SQUARE/FFN matmuls (at parity) — batched attn matmuls (batch=96, K=64 small) are catastrophic (deduceMMASchedule tiles batch to 1 + tiny MN => latency-bound, per the BMM_BOOST comment). In vit QK+PV ~444ms fork vs ~67ms jm per layer-set => ~15% of vit @ 7x too slow; +backward dK/dV/dQ (also batched small-K) doubles it. THE board-wide lever: fix batched-small-K attn matmul tiling (bigger MN/batch tiles to hide latency). BMM_BOOST tried this but failed (divisibility 592, -4% gpt2) — impl flawed, direction right. Est win: vit +8-15%, board-wide. NEXT: dump QK batched-matmul config (confirm tiny tiles) + fix deduceMMASchedule batched-small-K tile selection.
cont815 ROOT CAUSE + EXISTING FIX GATES: QK batched attn matmul (dispatch_20, 96x592x592x64) uses SPIRVBaseVectorize (SCALAR, tile[1,16,16], NO matrix units) => 0.17 TFLOP/s. setCooperativeMatrixConfig REJECTS it: bf16-OUTPUT attn matmuls fail coop unless canUpcastAcc (gated IREE_METAL_COOP_UPCAST_ACC, +needs dims>=128), and mustBeAligned=true rejects non-mult-16 dims (gated IREE_METAL_COOP_UNALIGNED, comment: "vit M=4616->scalar 0.31 vs jax-metal 2.51"). BOTH fixes EXIST env-gated OFF (pending numerical validation). Testing UNALIGNED + UPCAST_ACC on vit (correctness GNORM + throughput) — if they put attn matmuls on matrix units correctly = the 6-7x board win. This is THE lever, gated fixes already written.
cont816 EDIT: KernelConfig.cpp canUpcastAcc gate — added IREE_METAL_COOP_UPCAST_SMALLK to bypass the dimM/N/K>=128 requirement, so batched small-K attn matmuls (QK K=64) can upcast bf16-out to f32-acc COOP (more precise). Building + will A/B: QK dispatch COOP? vit GNORM(correct?) + throughput vs 0.94 baseline.
cont817 PROGRESS on THE lever (batched attn matmul -> coop): IREE_METAL_COOP_UPCAST_SMALLK gate WORKS — QK 592x592x64 now selects COOP (was SCALAR). But next codegen layer fails: 'gpu.subgroup_mma_constant_matrix got bf16' — the C-accumulator init constant is bf16, but WMMAR4_F32_16x16x16_BF16 needs f32 accumulator. Upcast promotes the accumulate but not the init constant. CLEAN FIX: promote attention QK/PV matmuls to f32-OUTPUT via RaiseContractionAccumulatorToF32 (already does this for FFN/most matmuls; skips attention+transposed-backward per cont815 comment) -> then clean f32-acc coop match, no bf16 constant. Genuine forward progress: lever confirmed (attn matmul is 6-7x gap), COOP selection achieved, one codegen layer left (f32-output promotion of attn matmuls). Edit is env-gated (harmless off). NEXT: extend RaiseContractionAccumulatorToF32 to attention matmuls, rebuild, measure vit.
cont818 ATTENTION-MATMUL lever FULLY RECONCILED: the f32-promote->coop path (RaiseContractionAccumulatorToF32, allowBatch DEFAULT-ON) already benefits ALIGNED models (banked in 67.1%). Measured aligned T=512 attn QK: fork 0.49 vs jm 1.16 TFLOP/s = still 2.4x behind (small-K=64 batched coop inherently less efficient than MPSGraph) + PV 1.12 vs 1.58. vit (577->pad 592) EXCLUDED by the pad-skip gate (line 123: f32-promoting padded scores = 141x drift; "future work: mask padded scores"). So the attention gap has TWO deep parts: (a) aligned small-K coop efficiency ~2x behind jm (deep matmul tuning), (b) vit pad-mask fix (deep, 1 model, +~2% board). My cont816 small-K UPCAST edit was the WRONG path (hit gpu.subgroup_mma_constant_matrix bf16 bug); reverted — the f32-PROMOTE path is right + already on. Tree clean @ HEAD, deployed dylib untouched. Both remaining levers deep; attention-coop for aligned already banked. Board 67.1%.
cont819 vit-attention lever EXHAUSTED (all env-gate routes tested): (1) f32-promote -> pad-skip gate excludes padded 592; (2) small-K UPCAST (cont816) -> COOP selected but gpu.subgroup_mma_constant_matrix bf16 codegen bug; (3) PAD_ATTN=0 + UNALIGNED -> CORRECT (GNORM 3.822555e5) but NO speedup (0.92 vs 0.93 — unaligned coop for 577 as slow as scalar, boundary-mask overhead). So vit's odd-seq(577) attention canNOT hit efficient coop on the current codegen via any gate. Remaining fixes all DEEP: the "future work" pad-mask (numerically-exact pad+promote), or a better odd-seq attention lowering, or closing the aligned small-K coop 2x gap. FINAL calibrated state: gap = batched small-K attention matmul coop efficiency; aligned models banked (~2x behind jm still), vit odd-seq stuck (7x). All quick/gated levers exhausted+measured. Board 67.1% stable, deployed dylib untouched, source @ HEAD, 10/10 correct, zero regressions ~186 turns. Next real progress = deep matmul-codegen (small-K batched coop) or vit pad-mask — dedicated multi-week work.
cont820 vit pad-mask fix precisely scoped (deep, cross-op): PadBatchMatmulToCoopPattern zero-pads {M,N,K} to mult-16. For QK, padding N(keys) with ZEROS -> fake score columns=0 -> softmax weights them -> 141x drift. Fix requires masking the fake key-score columns to -inf BEFORE softmax = cross-op coordination between the isolated pad pattern and the downstream softmax generic (a matmul can't emit -inf via padding) = genuinely "future work". (FFN 2D PadMatmulToCoopPattern IS validated+banked: vit FFN M=4616 0.31->0.66 2.1x.) TERMINUS: every gated/quick lever exhausted+measured across ~190 turns; gap = batched small-K attention matmul coop efficiency (aligned ~2x behind jm, banked; vit odd-seq 577 stuck 7x, needs cross-op pad-mask). Remaining = deep multi-week codegen (pad-mask cross-op masking OR small-K batched-coop tuning). Board 67.1% stable, dylib untouched, source @ HEAD, 10/10 correct, zero regressions. Campaign correctly aimed + fully de-risked of false leads.
